    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `clean_orders` in `app/clean.py`, and the one existing test passes (`pytest -q`: 1 passed). That test only checks that the function exists, so I also ran it on some sample frames and the output was correct.\n\n**What it does:**\n- **Works on a copy:** it runs `df.copy()` first, and I confirmed the input frame is unchanged afterwards.\n- **Text columns:** since pandas 3.0.6 is pinned, `read_csv` gives text columns the new `str` dtype rather than `object`. It handles both:\n  - `str` columns get trimmed, and empty strings become missing values.\n  - `object` columns may hold a mix of types, so it only trims the actual strings and leaves other values (numbers, `None`) as they are.\n  - Values that are empty after trimming (like `\" \"`) become missing.\n- **`qty`:** missing values become 0. With the sample CSV, `qty` loads as float64, so the filled values show as `0.0`.\n- Every other column is untouched.\n\n**Sample results:**\n- `\"  A1 \"` \u2192 `\"A1\"`\n- `\" \"` \u2192 NaN\n- the number `5` in a mixed text column stays `5`\n- missing `qty` \u2192 0.0\n- a nullable `\"string\"` column gives `<NA>` for blanks\n\n**Cleanup needed:** I left a check script at `.scratch/check.py`. Deleting it needed your approval, so it's still there. You can delete it; nothing depends on it."
